findings.problem:
The coin-toss overlay drops ALL of its controls on an early return keyed to PauseMenu.Paused, and the comment justifying it is factually wrong.

  CupCoinToss.cs:602-611
    void Draw()
    {
        // Under the pause menu the overlay hides: both draw at IMGUI depth 0 in no fixed
        // order, and a click blocker beneath the menu's buttons would eat their clicks. The
        // condition changes only in Update, so Layout and Repaint always agree.
        if (_stage == Stage.Done || PauseMenu.Paused) return;
        MenuScale.Begin();
        try { DrawInner(); }
        finally { MenuScale.End(); }
    }

DrawInner allocates three real controls, and does so with textbook care once it is reached (CupCoinToss.cs:625 `UITheme.ClickBlocker(w, h, heads, tails)` unconditionally, then CupCoinToss.cs:636-640 `GUI.enabled = showButtons; DrawCallButton(heads, ...); DrawCallButton(tails, ...);` — each DrawCallButton is a `UITheme.Button` at CupCoinToss.cs:654, and the comment at CupCoinToss.cs:620-622 even says "parked off-screen and disabled (never skipped - control ids)"). The outer guard throws that discipline away wholesale.

The premise is false. PauseMenu.Paused is written from inside an IMGUI event pass:
  PauseMenu.cs:407-410  bool hit = UITheme.Button(r, _entries[i].Label, btn);
                        if (hit) { Activate(_entries[i]); break; }
  PauseMenu.cs:248-259  Activate -> e.Act?.Invoke()  (the Resume entry, PauseMenu.cs:269)
  PauseMenu.cs:210-212  void Resume() { Paused = false; ... }
and also from PauseMenu.cs:459-460 (`DrawConfirm`'s Cancel/Confirm -> ClearConfirm/act) and PauseMenu.cs:298-302 (the cup's quit entry -> Unfreeze() -> `Paused = false` at PauseMenu.cs:229).

findings.fix:
Move the Paused test inside, next to the existing showButtons logic, so the control set is invariant:

  void Draw()
  {
      if (_stage == Stage.Done) return;   // _stage only moves in Update/Finish - safe
      MenuScale.Begin();
      try { DrawInner(); }
      finally { MenuScale.End(); }
  }

and in DrawInner make `bool showButtons = calling && LocalCanCall && !PauseMenu.Paused;` (CupCoinToss.cs:618) plus wrap the decorative draws (header/hint/tag/band) in `if (!PauseMenu.Paused)`. ClickBlocker and both buttons then stay allocated on every pass; the parked rects already put them off-screen and GUI.enabled already makes them inert, which is exactly the "hide under the menu" the comment wanted. The ClickBlocker's own `GUI.enabled = true` (UITheme.cs:981) means it would still block, so the paused case additionally needs the blocker parked — pass it the full-screen rect as a hole, e.g. `UITheme.ClickBlocker(w, h, PauseMenu.Paused ? new Rect(0f, 0f, w, h) : heads, tails)`, which keeps the control allocated and hands it a rect the mouse is always inside, so it neither responds nor consumes.

SIDE EFFECT: this file only; nothing outside the cup reads CupCoinToss. It does NOT fix the underlying PauseMenu.cs behaviour, so the same class of bug stays possible in any future screen that guards controls on Paused. If you would rather fix it once at the source, deferring PauseMenu's Activate/Resume to the end of its own OnGUI (a latched `System.Action` fired after MenuScale.End, the pattern every cup screen already uses) would make the comment true everywhere — but that touches the shared pause menu for all seven modes and should be its own change.

findings.problem:
The Head-to-Head watch bar drops two or three real buttons on the same false premise.

  CupDirector.HeadToHead.cs:1177-1183
    void H2HDrawWatchBar()
    {
        var d = Driver;
        var r = CurrentRound;
        // The condition only changes in Update (a driver comes and goes between frames), so
        // Layout and Repaint always agree on whether these controls exist.
        if (!_h2hHostRound || d == null || d.Setup == null || d.Setup.LocalHasBody || r == null || Bracket == null || PauseMenu.Paused) return;

The comment is accurate for every term EXCEPT `PauseMenu.Paused`, which (see the CupCoinToss finding for the exact call chain, PauseMenu.cs:407-410 -> Activate -> Resume -> PauseMenu.cs:212) is written from inside an IMGUI event pass. The controls it guards are real:

  CupDirector.HeadToHead.cs:1209-1215
    for (int i = 0; i < labels.Count; i++)
    {
        var br = new Rect(x + i * (bw + gap), y, bw, bh);
        var keep = GUI.backgroundColor;
        if (lit[i]) GUI.backgroundColor = UITheme.SelTint;
        if (UITheme.Button(br, labels[i], _h2hWatchBtn) && !lit[i]) fire = acts[i];
        GUI.backgroundColor = keep;
    }

This one is worse than the coin toss's because it is hooked FIRST and therefore drawn FIRST: CupDirector.HeadToHead.cs:408 `H2HHookWatchBar()` runs in H2HPrepareWave, before the toss's `AddGuiHook(Draw)` at CupCoinToss.cs:228. Hooks are invoked in list order (CupDirector.cs:545 `for (int i = 0; i < OnGuiHook.Count; i++) OnGuiHook[i]?.Invoke();`). So when Paused flips mid-pass, the watch bar's 2-3 button ids reappear ahead of the coin overlay's ClickBlocker and both HEADS/TAILS buttons, shifting all of them. A watcher who unpauses during a head-to-head coin toss can find the click landing on the wrong control, and this is exactly the state design 6.11 puts them in ("in Head to Head any spectator already watching" calls the coin).

findings.fix:
Same shape as the coin-toss fix — keep the structural terms in the guard, move only `PauseMenu.Paused` inside as a GUI.enabled + parked-rect condition:

  if (!_h2hHostRound || d == null || d.Setup == null || d.Setup.LocalHasBody || r == null || Bracket == null) return;
  ...
  bool live = !PauseMenu.Paused;
  GUI.enabled = live;
  for (int i = 0; i < labels.Count; i++)
  {
      var br = live ? new Rect(x + i * (bw + gap), y, bw, bh) : new Rect(-1000f, -1000f, bw, bh);
      ...
      if (UITheme.Button(br, labels[i], _h2hWatchBtn) && live && !lit[i]) fire = acts[i];
  }
  GUI.enabled = true;

and skip only the decorative UITheme.Hint at :1220 while paused.

Note the labels/acts/lit lists (CupDirector.HeadToHead.cs:1192-1207) are built INSIDE the GUI pass and their length depends on `e.IsHuman` per side — those terms genuinely only move between frames (the bracket entrants of a standing round do not change mid-pass), so that part is safe as it stands, though moving it to a model latched in H2HPrepareWave would match what CupLobbyUI/CupOrderUI/CupResultsUI/CupPodium already do and would stop allocating three Lists plus closures on every pass.

SIDE EFFECT: none outside the cup — H2HDrawWatchBar is private and hooked only from H2HPrepareWave.

findings.problem:
`UpdateLiveRow()` writes the local player's `Playing` / live scores / kick number straight onto the model and never calls `Notify()`:

    1727: public void UpdateLiveRow()
    1728: {
    1729:     var me = LocalPlayer;
    1730:     if (me == null || Driver == null || CurrentRound == null || me.Entrant < 0 || !CurrentRound.Involves(me.Entrant)) return;
    ...
    1734:     me.SetLive(Bracket.Entrants[CurrentRound.OpponentOf(me.Entrant)].NationIndex, own, theirs, Driver.KickIndex + 1);
    1735: }

A CLIENT is unaffected: `NetTickLiveRow` (CupDirector.Net.cs:726-743) polls `me.Playing` / `me.LiveScoreFor` / ... directly and sends `CupRequest.LiveRow`, whose host handler `ApplyLiveRow` (CupDirector.Net.cs:241-249) ends in `Notify()`.

The HOST has no such path — its own row is local — and `Notify()` is the ONLY thing that sets `_stateDirty` (CupDirector.Net.cs:283) and therefore the only thing that produces a `CupState` broadcast; `NetFlushState` returns at line 290 on `!_stateDirty && !force && !_stateForce` and there is no heartbeat. During the host's own Head to Head parallel round the tick is `H2HTickRound` -> `H2HTickLocalFlow` case Playing -> `UpdateLiveRow()` (HeadToHead.cs:600) and then `H2HHostAdvance(true)`, whose only Notify sources are `SimulateAiRounds` (`if (n > 0) Notify();` CupDirector.cs:1481 — n is 0 after the first frame) and `H2HResolveParallelCalls` (`if (changed) Notify();` HeadToHead.cs:1031). So no CupState leaves the host for the whole round.

On every client the host's row therefore still carries `Playing == false` from the last `ClearLive()`, so `CupLobbyUI.StatusOf` falls through to lines 356-361 and prints "Up next vs XXX" for the whole round instead of the live score, and `canSpectate` at CupLobbyUI.cs:302 (`h2h && !isMe && p.Active && p.Playing`) is false, so the Spectate button on the host's row is parked off-screen and disabled the entire time. Spectating the host is unreachable in Head to Head. (A HOST ROUND is only partly affected: `H2HSetHostRoundLive` calls `ApplyLiveRow` for the REMOTE participant, which Notifies on each change, so the host's own row rides along once a kick.)

When the round ends with the emote wheel open, the HUD re-CAPTURES the cursor immediately after the driver has deliberately freed it, because the two run in the wrong order off the same SetPhase.

  CupHud.cs:132-138
    void OnPhase(RoundPhase phase)
    {
        ...
        if ((phase == RoundPhase.Over || phase == RoundPhase.Idle) && _wheelOpen) SetWheel(false, true);
    }

  CupHud.cs:275-280
    void SetWheel(bool open, bool touchCursor)
    {
        if (touchCursor) CupEmoteWheel.SetOpen(ref _wheelOpen, open);
        ...

  CupEmoteWheel.cs:64-69
    public static void SetOpen(ref bool open, bool value)
    {
        KeepAlive(open || value);
        open = value;
        GameInput.CaptureCursor(!value);   // value == false  =>  CaptureCursor(TRUE)
    }

The driver's own Over handling frees it first, then this undoes it:

  CupRoundDriver.cs:355-363
    public void SetPhase(RoundPhase next)
    {
        ...
        OnPhaseChanged(prev, next);     // (1)
        PhaseChanged?.Invoke(next);     // (2)

  (1) reaches CupRoundDriver.Kick.cs:249-250  `case RoundPhase.Over: EndRoundVisuals(); break;`
      -> CupRoundDriver.Kick.cs:1073  `if (_cursorCaptured) { GameInput.CaptureCursor(false); _cursorCaptured = false; }`
      (its own doc comment, Kick.cs:1060: "the cursor is freed for the screen that follows")
  (2) reaches CupHud.OnPhase -> SetWheel(false, true) -> CaptureCursor(true).

So (2) wins and the cursor is locked going into the screen that follows. This is a genuine ordering inversion, not a style point.

